{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And if a man strikes his slave or his maidservant.</b> The verse speaks of a Canaanite slave. Or is it not rather a Hebrew slave? Scripture says, ״כִּי כַסְפּוֹ הוּא״ — just as 'his money' is his permanently, so too a slave acquired permanently. But was it not already included in 'one who strikes a man and he dies'? Rather, the verse comes and removes it from the general rule, to be judged by the law of a day or two — that if he did not die under his hand and survived from time to time, he is exempt (Mekhilta)." "<b>With a rod.</b> The verse speaks of one that has enough to kill. Or even one that does not have enough to kill? Scripture says, concerning an Israelite: ״וְאִם בְּאֶבֶן יָד אֲשֶׁר יָמוּת בָּהּ הִכָּהוּ״ (Numbers 35) — and is this not a fortiori: if an Israelite, the more stringent case, one is liable only if he struck him with something that has enough to kill and on a limb that is liable to die from this blow — then a slave, the less stringent case, is it not all the more so? (Mekhilta)." "<b>Avenged, he shall be avenged.</b> Death by the sword. And so it says: ״חֶרֶב נֹקֶמֶת נְקַם בְּרִית״ (Leviticus 26)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:39.603172578Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}